eeds (problem-class \\u2192 template) as documented; report how many templates per benchmark.\\n4. **Checkmate-in-One representation** \\u2014 recommend FEN (compact, structural); pure-LLM-readable board is also possible. Latency profile differs (FEN \\u2192 fewer tokens \\u2192 faster LLM call, but parse step adds bookkeeping). Default to FEN to match standard usage; flag if user prefers otherwise.\\n5. **PAL execution environment** \\u2014 sandboxed subprocess via `bwrap` or `resource`+`signal` caps (CPU/mem/walltime cap, no network, no fs write outside tmp); pure stdlib Python invoked with `-I -S` for determinism.\\n6. **Paired vs unpaired comparison** \\u2014 paired on problem identity is feasible and recommended (each method sees the same problems); paired Wilcoxon signed-rank for latency (distributions are right-skewed, hence non-parametric); effect size = per-problem latency ratio (e.g., `t_BoT / t_Expert`); 95 % CI via BCa bootstrap over problems.\\n\\n### Statistical design\\n- Sample size: per benchmark use the standard published problem set (Game of 24 = 30\\u201350 puzzles, MGSM = the paper's standard subset, Checkmate = standard Lichess-derived set). K=5 repeats per (problem, method) cell to estimate within-cell variance from sampling non-determinism (assumes sampling temperature > 0; if temperature = 0, K=1 still gives a deterministic point estimate and K>1 only re-confirms; happy to set K=3 if LLM-call budget is tighter).\\n- Pre-registered contrasts: Expert vs PAL, Expert vs BoT, PAL vs BoT, **stratified by benchmark**.\\n- Power note: with ~30 problems per benchmark and a paired design, detecting a 20\\u201330 % latency ratio at \\u03b1=0.05, power=0.8 is feasible for typical effect sizes reported in the literature; if smaller effects are expected this needs flagging.\\n\\n### Analysis plan skeleton\\n- Per cell: median, IQR, mean, SD, max latency; per-component decomposition table.\\n- Cost-tree figure: stacked-bar of components per method per benc