{"id": "6b9cbc0d-0d6f-496b-9421-f262859ba852", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T09:42:09.175944", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d489735c-1cf2-49e3-8bd1-7f79393e7625", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design — Does Buffer of Thoughts (BoT) Increase Inference Time vs Expert and PAL across Game of 24, MGSM, and Checkmate-in-One?\n\n## 0. Framing and execution caveat (read first)\n\n- **This is a design-only deliverable.** No experiment has been executed, and — given the environment (no network, no LLM API keys, no local model, no chess engine, no `python-chess`, no `numpy`/scientific Python) — **no experiment could be executed in this workspace**. Any numerics in this document are **PARAMETERIZED — NOT MEASURED**, clearly labelled with origin. This is consistent with `track.md`: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"*\n- The design answers the **research question**, not a specific hypothesis about whether BoT is *worth its cost*: that is a separate accuracy-per-latency analysis and is flagged in §8 as out of scope.\n- All non-trivial numbers carry a `Source` column: **(P)** = BoT paper mechanism / Section / Table reference, **(C)** = public third-party API pricing/latency documentation, **(I)** = illustrative assumption explicitly for the design.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question, hypotheses (structural)\n\n**RQ.** Does BoT increase inference time vs single-query Expert (CoT) and single-query PAL across Game of 24, MGSM, and Checkmate-in-One?\n\n**Structural hypotheses (derived only from BoT's published mechanism, not from measured data).**\n| ID | Hypothesis | Source for structure |\n|----|------------|----------------------|\n| H1 | Per-query wall-clock: BoT > Expert in steady state. | (P) BoT issues ≥ 2 LLM round-trips per query; Expert issues 1. (cf. setup_7 vs setup_9) |\n| H2 | Per-query wall-clock: BoT > PAL in steady state (under native comparability). | (P) BoT adds retrieval + 1 extra LLM round-trip vs PAL's 1 LLM round-trip. |\n| H3 | Cold-start BoT > amortized BoT per query; gap dominated by first-fill buffer cost. | (P) Buffer pre-population is one-time; subsequent queries pay only retrieval + template instantiation. |\n| H4 | Overhead ratio `t_BoT / t_Expert` is largest for Checkmate-in-One (longer prompts, more LLM tokens) and smallest for MGSM (compact prompts). | (P) Token count is a near-linear term in latency for autoregressive decoding. |\n| H5 | Token cost: BoT > Expert ≈ PAL (input-heavy prompts for retrieval context + template). | (P) BoT prepends retrieved template to the prompt. |\n\nH5 ⇒ BoT's overhead is **structural, not implementation-driven**; even an idealized implementation is expected to obey H1–H3.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Variables\n\n### 2.1 Independent variables (factorial, 3 × 3)\n- **Method** ∈ {Expert, PAL, BoT}\n  - Expert = single-query CoT → **setup_7**\n  - PAL = single-query code-augmented → **setup_8**\n  - BoT = template-guided retrieval-augmented reasoning → **setup_9**\n- **Benchmark** ∈ {Game of 24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One}\n\n### 2.2 Dependent variables (layered; primary is bolded)\n- **Primary: end-to-end wall-clock latency per query, in seconds, steady-state (amortized).**\n- Secondary component decomposition (per query):\n  - Expert → `t_LLM_cot`\n  - PAL → `t_LLM_code` + `t_exec`\n  - BoT → `t_embed` + `t_buffer_lookup` + `t_instance_gen` + `t_LLM_final` + `t_bookkeeping`\n- Secondary: total input + output tokens per query.\n- Secondary: number of LLM round-trips per query.\n- Sanity covariate (not a competing DV): accuracy (proportion of problems solved correctly per cell).\n\n### 2.3 Cold-start vs amortized — both reported (§4.4)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Materials\n\n### 3.1 Datasets (sourced from BoT and predecessor papers)\n\n| Benchmark | Origin | Construction used by BoT paper | Notes |\n|-----------|--------|--------------------------------|-------|\n| Game of 24 | (P) Yao et al., 2023 (Tree of Thoughts) 4-number arithmetic puzzles | Standard published puzzle list; per BoT paper Section/Table on G24: subset is small but enumeration-heavy | Brute-search-friendly → PAL has measurably non-trivial `t_exec` |\n| MGSM | (P) Shi et al., 2022 (MGSM); subset used in BoT evaluation | Multilingual grade-school math; per-problem ground-truth integer answer | Compact prompts; short outputs |\n| Checkmate-in-One | (P) Lichess-derived puzzle set curated for short-horizon tactical reasoning | Single-move-mate puzzles; FEN representation is the standard textual form | Longest prompts of the three; structured board representation |\n\nSample-size policy (per **U3**): adopt standard published problem-set sizes as documented in those papers; report **post-hoc achieved power** for the paired test rather than fixing N by power calculation. Per-problem repeat count is fixed at K = 5 (A4) to estimate sampling variance.\n\n### 3.2 Model deployment target (per **A2 / U2**)\n- **Canonical** target: GPT-4-class latency profile. Treat `t_LLM ≈ α + β · tokens_out + γ · tokens_in`.\n- **Tier-swap table** provided in §7.2 for sensitivity (GPT-3.5-class, GPT-4-mini-class).\n\n### 3.3 BoT task-conditioned buffer (per **A5**)\n- Per BoT paper, each benchmark gets a task-specific meta-buffer.\n- Buffer initialization uses the paper's seed templates (problem-class → reasoning-template). Avoid generic/random init (A5).\n- Anchor for *template-reuse* discussion: **setup_29**.\n\n### 3.4 PAL execution environment (per **A8**)\n- Sandboxed Python interpreter. Two acceptable implementations:\n  - `bwrap --ro-bind /usr --bind /tmp --tmpfs /home --unshare-net --timeout 30 -- python3 -I -S <script>`\n  - `subprocess.run([...], preexec_fn=...)` + `resource.setrlimit` for CPU/mem/walltime; `signal.SIGALRM` for hard kill.\n- No network, no file writes outside `tmpfs`. Walltime cap ≈ 30 s per script (configurable per benchmark).\n\n### 3.5 Checkmate-in-One representation (per **A6**)\n- **FEN** as the default textual board state. Free-text board allowed only as sensitivity (reported separately if used). Anchor block for single-query comparators: **setup_28**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Procedure\n\n### 4.1 Method implementations (operational contracts)\n\n**Expert (setup_7).** Single LLM round-trip with a chain-of-thought prompt. No code execution, no retrieval. Timer starts at request send; timer ends at completion of response. Output is parsed; correctness judged against ground truth.\n\n**PAL (setup_8).** Single LLM round-trip prompting the model to emit a self-contained Python program. The program is executed in the §3.4 sandbox. Timer covers both `t_LLM_code` and `t_exec`. Output is the program's printed value; judged against ground truth.\n\n**BoT (setup_9 with setup_29 template reuse).**\n1. Compute the problem's embedding vector `e` (a deterministic function of the problem text; embedding model is fixed and ideally identical to the one used in the BoT reference pipeline).\n2. Retrieve top-`k` templates from the task-conditioned buffer by cosine similarity (parameter `k` from BoT reference, typically small).\n3. `t_instance_gen`: an LLM call that takes the retrieved template and instantiates it with the problem-specific values (fills variables, fixes placeholders).\n4. `t_LLM_final`: a second LLM call that takes the instantiated template and the problem to produce the final answer.\n5. `t_bookkeeping`: retrieval orchestration (e.g., JSON marshalling, kNN latency), measured but expected to be small.\n\n### 4.2 Comparability (per **A7**) — dual reporting\n\n| Comparability regime | Description |\n|----------------------|-------------|\n| **Fixed CoT budget** | All methods receive the same prompt context window allowance and same number of allowed steps. Apples-to-apples mechanism isolation. |\n| **Native form** | Each method is run in its canonical form (BoT with retrieval + 2 LLM calls; PAL with code exec; Expert with single CoT). Reflects realistic deployment. |\n\nBoth regimes are computed and reported side-by-side as a sensitivity table (§7.3). The headline result section surfaces the *native form* result.\n\n### 4.3 Per-query timing protocol\n- **Wall-clock** timer uses `time.perf_counter()` (Python stdlib) anchored at request-send and at response-complete (or, for component decomposition, between segment boundaries).\n- All times logged to a per-query record: `problem_id, method, regime, repeat_idx, t_total, t_components, tokens_in, tokens_out, correct, metadata`.\n- Per-method component decomposition is logged explicitly so the cost-tree figure can be reproduced from the log.\n\n### 4.4 Cold-start vs amortized handling (per **U4**)\n\n- **Cold-start metric**: first query against a fresh buffer triggers a one-time `t_buffer_init` (loading templates, building the index). This time is **recorded separately** and **not folded into steady-state DVs**.\n- **Amortized metric**: for queries 2..N, the per-query latency excludes `t_buffer_init` and is the headline steady-state DV.\n- **Reporting**: cold-start and amortized are separate columns/rows in all tables (see §7). Cold-start is *not* a confound in the headline comparison because Expert and PAL have no analogous first-query cost; it is reported as supplementary.\n\n### 4.5 Sampling non-determinism (per **A4**)\n- LLM decoding uses sampling with temperature and top-p fixed across methods for a given experiment.\n- K = 5 repeats per (problem, method, regime). Per-problem median (or mean if distribution is sufficiently tight) is the per-cell unit for paired tests.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Statistical design\n\n### 5.1 Pre-registered contrasts (per researcher's request — 18 rows = 3 benchmarks × 3 method pairs × 2 starting-states)\n\n| ID | Benchmark | Method A | Method B | Starting state | Test | α | Effect size | CI |\n|----|-----------|----------|----------|----------------|------|---|-------------|----|\n| C01 | G24 | Expert | PAL | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | per-problem latency ratio `t_PAL/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C02 | G24 | Expert | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C03 | G24 | PAL | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C04 | MGSM | Expert | PAL | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_PAL/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C05 | MGSM | Expert | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C06 | MGSM | PAL | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C07 | Checkmate | Expert | PAL | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_PAL/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C08 | Checkmate | Expert | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C09 | Checkmate | PAL | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C10 | G24 | Expert | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C11 | G24 | PAL | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C12 | MGSM | Expert | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C13 | MGSM | PAL | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C14 | Checkmate | Expert | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C15 | Checkmate | PAL | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\n| C16 | G24 | Expert | PAL | cold | (≈ steady; identical cold/ steady) | — | — | — |\n| C17 | MGSM | Expert | PAL | cold | (≈ steady) | — | — | — |\n| C18 | Checkmate | Expert | PAL | cold | (≈ steady) | — | — | — |\n\nNotes. C16–C18 are listed for completeness but are degenerate (Expert and PAL have no cold-start analogue for the buffer); they are reported explicitly to avoid implying an asymmetry.\n\n### 5.2 Power/achievable-effect (per **U3**)\n- Post-hoc achieved power for each contrast is reported using the Wilcoxon paired signed-rank normal approximation at the observed effect (latency ratio) and observed N per benchmark.\n- For design-time expectations (illustrative): paired Wilcoxon with N ≈ 30 problems per benchmark and K = 5 repeats → ~150 paired observations per contrast can typically detect ≥ 20–30 % latency ratios at α = 0.05, power ≈ 0.8. Smaller effects (e.g., < 15 %) may be underpowered at these N. **(I) Illustrative; depends on empirical effect size.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis plan\n\n### 6.1 Per-cell descriptive table (template)\n\n| Cell | n problems | K | Median t_total (s) | IQR | Mean t_total (s) | SD | Token in (mean) | Token out (mean) | RT count | Accuracy (mean ± 95 % CI) |\n|------|-----------|---|-------------------|-----|------------------|----|-----------------|------------------|----------|---------------------------|\n\n### 6.2 Component-decomposition table (per benchmark, in `ms`)\n(Banner: **PARAMETERIZED — NOT MEASURED** — see §7.2 for source column.)\n\n| Benchmark | Method | t_embed | t_buffer_lookup | t_instance_gen | t_LLM_final | t_bookkeeping | t_LLM_code | t_exec | **t_total** |\n|-----------|--------|---------|------------------|----------------|-------------|---------------|------------|--------|-------------|\n| G24 | Expert | — | — | — | — | — | — | — | *MS* |\n| G24 | PAL | — | — | — | — | — | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* |\n| G24 | BoT | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | — | — | *MS* |\n| MGSM | Expert | — | — | — | — | — | — | — | *MS* |\n| MGSM | PAL | — | — | — | — | — | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* |\n| MGSM | BoT | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | — | — | *MS* |\n| Checkmate | Expert | — | — | — | — | — | — | — | *MS* |\n| Checkmate | PAL | — | — | — | — | — | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* |\n| Checkmate | BoT | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | — | — | *MS* |\n\n*MS = empirically measured values (placeholder pending execution).*\n\n### 6.3 Cost-tree stacked-bar figure — textual specification\n\n**Figure name.** `cost_tree_<benchmark>.png` (one figure per benchmark; in the sensitivity report, also `<regime>` and `<tier>` variants).\n\n**Layout.**\n- X-axis: method ∈ {Expert, PAL, BoT} (categorical, three groups, evenly spaced).\n- Y-axis: per-query wall-clock seconds, **log scale**, with gridlines at 1 s, 10 s. Y-axis label: \"Per-query steady-state wall-clock latency (s, log)\".\n- Bars: stacked, one bar per method, bar height = `t_total`.\n- Color stack (bottom→top):\n  - **Expert:** single solid color \"LLM call\" (α + β·tokens).\n  - **PAL:** bottom = \"Code execution\"; top = \"LLM code-emit call\".\n  - **BoT:** bottom = \"Embedding\"; then \"Buffer lookup\"; then \"Template instantiation LLM\"; then \"Final reasoning LLM\"; sliver = \"Bookkeeping\".\n- Legend to the right; one swatch per component; consistent palette across the three sub-figures.\n- Annotations: mean `t_total` printed above each bar; ratio `t_BoT / t_Expert` printed next to the BoT bar.\n- Expected proportions (illustrative — **(I)**; banner applies per §7.2):\n  - G24: PAL `t_exec` dominates (large brute-search); BoT components roughly equal except `t_LLM_final` slightly larger.\n  - MGSM: all methods compact; `t_exec` tiny in PAL; BoT overhead concentrated in the two LLM round-trips.\n  - Checkmate: BoT's two LLM round-trips dominate the bar; embedding/lookup are thin slices; PAL `t_exec` may be non-trivial if a chess-engine surrogate is in-voked.\n\n**Justification.** The cost-tree figure is *the* single artifact most directly answering the research question; each method's bar makes the structural driver of any latency ratio visible at a glance.\n\n### 6.4 Inferential\n- Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test per contrast in §5.1.\n- Effect size = per-problem latency ratio.\n- 95 % CI via BCa bootstrap over problems (B = 10 000 resamples).\n- Holm–Bonferroni correction within each benchmark family (3 contrasts / benchmark / regime) to control family-wise error.\n- Achieved power reported per contrast (see §5.2).\n\n### 6.5 Sanity plots\n- Accuracy × latency scatter (per method, per benchmark). If a method sits at near-zero accuracy, omit it from latency comparisons or annotate prominently — latency of a broken solver is not a comparable quantity.\n- Per-problem latency histogram (per cell), to motivate the non-parametric test choice.\n\n### 6.6 Template-reuse amortization sub-analysis (per **setup_29**)\n- Within the BoT cell, partition queries by *n_reuses* of the retrieved template (how many other problems in the buffer hit a near-identical template). Compare per-query latency across `n_reuses` buckets. **Expected direction (structural):** per-query latency decreases modestly as `n_reuses` grows because (a) the same template path is cached, (b) the embedding/lookup path may short-circuit on cache hit. Report as a sub-figure alongside the main ANOVA.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Parameterized reference tables\n\n> **PARAMETERIZED — NOT MEASURED.** Numbers below are **order-of-magnitude reference values** derived from third-party latency documentation, BoT paper's mechanism, and reasonable assumptions. They are not empirical measurements. Source column coding: **(P)** BoT paper mechanism / Section / Table reference; **(C)** public third-party pricing/latency docs; **(I)** illustrative assumption made explicitly for this design.\n\n### 7.1 Tier-swap (per **U2**)\n\n| Tier | α — per-call fixed (s) | β_in — per input token (ms) | β_out — per output token (ms) | γ — embedding latency (ms) | δ — kNN lookup (ms) | Source |\n|------|------------------------|------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------|--------|\n| GPT-4-class (canonical) | 0.5 – 1.2 | 0.03 – 0.06 | 0.06 – 0.12 | 50 – 200 | 1 – 50 | (C) broad public API latency bands |\n| GPT-4-mini-class | 0.3 – 0.7 | 0.005 – 0.02 | 0.02 – 0.05 | 30 – 100 | 1 – 50 | (C) public docs |\n| GPT-3.5-class | 0.2 – 0.5 | 0.001 – 0.005 | 0.005 – 0.02 | 20 – 80 | 1 – 50 | (C) public docs |\n\n(P) Per BoT paper, the dominant latency term in retrieval-augmented decoding is the per-token output term; α is a one-per-call overhead.\n\n### 7.2 Illustrative template for the headline ratio table\n\n| Benchmark | Method | Median t_total (s) | IQR (s) | Token out (mean) | RT count | Source |\n|-----------|--------|---------------------|---------|-----------------|----------|--------|\n| G24 | Expert | \"[Param 4–8 s]\" | \"[Param 2–5 s]\" | \"[Param 80–250]\" | 1 | (I) prompt ≈ 200 tok in / 200 tok out |\n| G24 | PAL | \"[Param 5–30 s]\" | \"[Param 5–20 s]\" | \"[Param 80–250]\" | 1 | (I) execution dominated by brute search |\n| G24 | BoT | \"[Param 8–18 s]\" | \"[Param 4–10 s]\" | \"[Param 200–500]\" | 2 | (I) two LLM calls, retrieval adds ms-scale |\n| MGSM | Expert | \"[Param 2–5 s]\" | \"[Param 1–3 s]\" | \"[Param 40–150]\" | 1 | (I) short prompts/outputs |\n| MGSM | PAL | \"[Param 2–6 s]\" | \"[Param 1–3 s]\" | \"[Param 40–150]\" | 1 | (I) execution ≈ ms |\n| MGSM | BoT | \"[Param 4–10 s]\" | \"[Param 2–5 s]\" | \"[Param 100–300]\" | 2 | (I) two LLM calls dominate |\n| Checkmate | Expert | \"[Param 5–12 s]\" | \"[Param 3–7 s]\" | \"[Param 150–400]\" | 1 | (I) longer FEN+reasoning prompts |\n| Checkmate | PAL | \"[Param 5–15 s]\" | \"[Param 3–8 s]\" | \"[Param 150–400]\" | 1 | (I) execution cost raises PAL overhead if engine subbed |\n| Checkmate | BoT | \"[Param 10–25 s]\" | \"[Param 5–12 s]\" | \"[Param 300–700]\" | 2 | (I) BoT bar dominated by two LLM calls |\n\nQuoted ranges are populated at runtime from §7.1 coefficients and per-benchmark prompt-length distributions; this template is illustrative scaffolding only.\n\n### 7.3 Comparability sensitivity table (native form vs fixed budget) — template dimensions\n\n| Benchmark | Pair | Median ratio (native form) | Median ratio (fixed budget) | Direction agrees? | Source |\n|-----------|------|----------------------------|------------------------------|--------------------|--------|\n| G24 | Exp vs PAL | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\n| G24 | Exp vs BoT | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\n| G24 | PAL vs BoT | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\n| MGSM | (same rows) | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\n| Checkmate | (same rows) | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\n\nIf direction disagrees across regimes, that is a *finding* about the design's comparability assumption, not an error.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Threats to validity (per researcher's request)\n\n| Threat | Description | Mitigation in the design |\n|--------|-------------|--------------------------|\n| **No-execution caveat** | This document is design-only; no experiment has been run. The environment cannot run it. | All numerics are PARAMETERIZED with a Source column; the design is fully specified as a research protocol ready to be handed to a compute-enabled lab; no claim of empirical results is made. |\n| **Sampling non-determinism** | LLM decoding is stochastic under sampling. Single-run latency is noisy. | K = 5 repeats per (problem, method); per-problem aggregate (median) used as the cell's paired-test unit. |\n| **Cold-start vs amortized confounding** | BoT's first query carries buffer-fill cost; subsequent queries do not. Folding cold-start into steady-state would overestimate BoT latency; folding it into Expert/PAL has no analogue. | Reported as separate columns/rows. Cold-start contrasts are pre-registered separately (C10–C15). |\n| **Model tier sensitivity** | Latency coefficients α, β depend on the deployment target model. Conclusions might differ by tier. | Tier-swap table (§7.1) gives α/β bands. Headline result reported at every tier; sign-consistency of the latency-ratio claim across tiers is the robustness criterion. |\n| **Comparability (fixed budget vs native form)** | Fixed-budget apples-to-apples may handicap BoT (which is naturally more expensive); native form reflects deployment but breaks mechanism isolation. | Dual reporting (§7.3). If direction disagrees across regimes, report explicitly. |\n| **Benchmark heterogeneity** | G24 (symbolic search), MGSM (numeric math), Checkmate-in-One (board reasoning) have different latency drivers; aggregating across benchmarks hides structure. | Stratified analysis per benchmark; no main effect across benchmarks reported as a single number. |\n| **Hardware / network variance** | Wall-clock depends on deployment substrate (datacenter vs. edge, network RTT). | The design reports component decomposition as the substrate-invariant component summary; wall-clock is reported with substrate metadata alongside every number. |\n| **Operator / orchestration overhead** | BoT's `t_bookkeeping` depends on the buffering system's implementation. A naïve Python-list `O(N)` lookup, for instance, can dominate at scale. | `t_bookkeeping` is bounded by a simple implementation note (≤ O(log N) for small buffers via a flat list; FAISS-class index for larger buffers). The design specifies a fixed budget for the buffer implementation. |\n| **PAL on chess** | PAL needs an executable form of chess reasoning; if it shells out to a stockfish-like engine, `t_exec` is large and may not be a fair native-form comparison. | For Checkmate-in-One, PAL is run in two variants: (i) generate Python that brute-searches legal moves; (ii) generate Python invoking a chess-engine surrogate inside the sandbox. Both are reported separately as a sensitivity row. |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Expected-direction discussion (grounded only in BoT's published mechanism)\n\n> **PARAMETERIZED — NOT MEASURED.** This section states the **direction** the design predicts, derived only from BoT's mechanism. No measured magnitudes are claimed.\n\n**Why BoT is expected to be slower per query than Expert and PAL.**\n1. **Round-trip count is the dominant term.** BoT issues ≥ 2 LLM round-trips (template instantiation + final reasoning) per query; Expert issues 1; PAL issues 1. Since per-call fixed overhead α is positive (typical range 0.3–1.2 s per §7.1), adding a second round-trip adds at minimum α tokens-decoded-plus-TTFB latency. This single structural fact is sufficient to predict **H1 and H2** from §1. (Anchor: setup_9 vs setup_7, setup_8.)\n2. **Retrieval and embedding are smaller but additive.** `t_embed + t_buffer_lookup` is expected to be on the order of tens to a few hundred milliseconds for small buffers (§7.1, γ + δ). These components are **not** latency-dominant but appear as the lower slices of the cost-tree bar.\n3. **Token output is the second-largest term.** The BoT pattern prepends the retrieved template to the prompt (input) and uses the template-guided LLM to fill in the answer (output). Per-benchmark token output is therefore larger than the bare Expert pattern. Combined with the second round-trip, this drives a non-trivial additional latency share.\n\n**Why the cold-start vs amortized gap matters.**\n- Cold-start BoT pays `t_buffer_init` once on the first query. For small problem sets this single-query contribution to the per-query mean is non-negligible. **Amortized** BoT excludes this and is the headline metric. If a practitioner uses BoT \"in anger\" on a small workload, cold-start can dominate; on a long workload, amortized latency is the relevant number. Splitting the two makes the deployment-relevant answer unambiguous.\n\n**Why the per-benchmark structure differs.**\n- **G24** is brute-search-heavy for PAL; `t_exec` is the largest PAL slice. BoT's overhead is mechanical (extra round-trip) but moderate because the LLM portion is moderate. Expect the BoT vs PAL gap to be smaller for G24 than for MGSM.\n- **MGSM** is prompt/output-compact; LLM call latency is small in absolute terms, but BoT's second round-trip is *relatively* more visible. Expect the largest relative overhead `t_BoT / t_Expert` here.\n- **Checkmate-in-One** has the longest prompts/outputs. BoT's prepended template inflates input tokens the most on this benchmark. Expect the largest *absolute* overhead here, but the ratio may be in the same band as MGSM because Expert is also longer.\n\n**Template-reuse (setup_29) sub-analysis.**\n- For repeated problem classes, the same template is reused across many queries. Per-query overhead that is caching-invariant (TTFB, decoding the first-pass template instantiation output) remains; per-query overhead that is cache-sensitive (e.g., retrieval scoring against a hot template, lookup-step caching) drops. The design's sub-analysis (§6.6) is the right place to see whether BoT's per-query latency genuinely approaches Expert's in the long-run steady state. **Predicted direction:** modest decrease as `n_reuses` grows, but BoT does **not** approach Expert latency in absolute terms because two LLM calls remain.\n\n**Net expected answer to the research question.**\n- **Steady-state per-query wall-clock:** BoT > Expert; BoT > PAL (in both regimes, sign-consistent across model tiers from §7.1).\n- **Cold-start:** the BoT vs {Expert, PAL} gap is larger because BoT additionally carries `t_buffer_init` on the first query only.\n- **Relative magnitudes** depend on the benchmark and model tier; the cost-tree figure is the artifact that makes this concrete.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Study-context anchors (citation block)\n\nThe design anchors its IV and method definitions to the following in-scope facts:\n\n- **setup_7** — Expert Prompting (single-query CoT baseline). The IV's Expert level is defined here: one LLM round-trip, no retrieval, no code execution.\n- **setup_8** — PAL (single-query code-augmented). The IV's PAL level is defined here: one LLM round-trip produces a self-contained Python program; the program runs in a sandbox.\n- **setup_9** — BoT (template-guided retrieval-augmented reasoning). The IV's BoT level is defined here: embedding + buffer lookup + template-instantiation LLM call + final-reasoning LLM call.\n- **setup_28** — Single-query comparators block. The grouping of Expert and PAL under \"single-query\" is anchored here; informs A7's dual-regime decision and §6.3's bar grouping.\n- **setup_29** — BoT template-reuse block. The discussion of long-run amortization and per-query overhead reduction is anchored here; motivates §6.6's template-reuse sub-analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 11. What is **NOT** in this design (honest scoping)\n\nOut of scope, flagged because an unwary reader might expect them:\n- **No accuracy-per-latency claim.** Accuracy is a sanity covariate, not a competing DV. A study of *\"is BoT cost-effective?\"* would need a different design (accuracy-per-second frontier), and is **not** the research question.\n- **No claim of an executed experiment.** This is design-only (§0).\n- **No measured numbers.** All numerics are PARAMETERIZED; the Source column tells you where each row came from (§7 banner).\n- **No claim of a particular deployment substrate.** Tier-swap parameters span realistic deployment bands (§7.1).\n- **No statistical inference without execution.** The inferential plan (§5, §6.4) is fully specified but its outputs are placeholders (`*MS*`) pending execution.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Compact summary block (for the researcher's quick review)\n\n| Item | Value |\n|------|-------|\n| Deliverable | Design-only research protocol + analysis plan + expected-direction discussion grounded in BoT mechanism; **no execution**; **no measured numbers** |\n| IV | Method ∈ {Expert, PAL, BoT} × Benchmark ∈ {G24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One} |\n| Primary DV | per-query wall-clock latency, steady-state, seconds |\n| Secondary DVs | component decomposition (per method), token in/out, LLM round-trip count |\n| Sanity covariate | accuracy |\n| Comparability regimes | both: fixed-budget and native form (dual reporting) |\n| Starting states | both: cold and steady-state BoT; single for Expert/PAL |\n| Pre-registered contrasts | 18 (15 informative + 3 degenerate Expert↔PAL-cold rows) |\n| Stat test | paired Wilcoxon signed-rank, α = 0.05, Holm-corrected within benchmark/regime |\n| Effect size | per-problem latency ratio; 95 % BCa bootstrap CI |\n| Repeats | K = 5 per (problem, method, regime) |\n| Model tiers considered | GPT-4-class (canonical), GPT-4-mini-class, GPT-3.5-class (α/β bands in §7.1) |\n| Headline artifact | cost-tree stacked-bar figure (§6.3), one per benchmark + sensitivity variants |\n| Anchored by | setup_7, setup_8, setup_9, setup_28, setup_29 |\n| Output numbers | none — every numeric is PARAMETERIZED with Source column |", "evidence": "Grounded in: (a) reconnaissance above — environment cannot execute LLM benchmarks; (b) the researcher's specific structural asks (cost-tree spec, 18-row contrast table, threats-to-validity, expected-direction from BoT mechanism only, anchors for setup_7/8/9/28/29); (c) the protocol's DV decomposition tracks the user's layered structure (wall-clock primary, components/tokens/round-trips secondary, accuracy covariate); (d) every numeric carries a PARAMETERIZED banner with a Source column coded (P) BoT paper mechanism, (C) public API latency docs, or (I) illustrative assumption; (e) the dual comparability regimes, cold/amortized split, tier-swap table, and 18 pre-registered contrasts directly reflect the researcher's A1–A10 and U1–U5 resolutions; (f) the threats-to-validity subsection explicitly covers the five concerns the researcher named plus operator/orchestration and PAL-on-chess sensitivities; (g) the expected-direction discussion cites only BoT's mechanism (round-trip count, retrieval overhead, prepended template tokens, template-reuse amortization), and the template-reuse sub-analysis is anchored to setup_29. No fabricated measured data: every empirical column is marked *MS*; every numeric range is labelled PARAMETERIZED.", "assumptions": ["A1 Inference time primary definition end to end wall clock per query steady state amortized with secondary component decomposition and tokens and round trips count", "A2 Same LLM held constant across methods for fairness with model identity parameterized in the design", "A3 Paired on problem identity within each benchmark", "A4 K equals 5 repeats per problem method to capture sampling variance include override flag", "A5 BoT uses original papers task conditioned meta buffer not random or generic initialization", "A6 Checkmate in One uses FEN representation as default with free text board as sensitivity", "A7 Comparability dual reporting of fixed CoT budget and native form results as a sensitivity row", "A8 PAL sandbox uses bwrap or stdlib subprocess with resource and signal caps no network no fs write outside tmp walltime cap", "A9 Effect size equals per problem latency ratio with BCa bootstrap CI significance test equals paired Wilcoxon Holm corrected within benchmark family", "A10 Deliverable is pure design document no mock simulator build PARAMETERIZED numerics allowed with banner and source column", "U1 Pure design only no mock build", "U2 Canonical target is GPT 4 class latency profile with tier swap table for GPT 3.5 and GPT 4 mini", "U3 Standard published problem set sizes adopt with post hoc achieved power report per contrast", "U4 Both cold start and amortized BoT are reported in separate columns rows", "U5 Parameterized numerics acceptable with mandatory PARAMETERIZED banner and source column for each numeric"], "uncertainties": ["U1 Whether deliverable should be design only or include built mock; resolved to design only", "U2 Which LLM tier is the canonical target; resolved to GPT 4 class with tier swap table", "U3 Pre register MDE vs adopt published set and report power; resolved to published set plus post hoc power", "U4 Cold start vs amortized; resolved to report both", "U5 Parameterized numerics acceptable; resolved with mandatory PARAMETERIZED banner and source column"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-cf421e78-6584-4db1-8082-8c48bba47964", "tool_call": {"id": "call-cf421e78-6584-4db1-8082-8c48bba47964", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Submit full design with anchors\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design \\u2014 Does Buffer of Thoughts (BoT) Increase Inference Time vs Expert and PAL across Game of 24, MGSM, and Checkmate-in-One?\\n\\n## 0. Framing and execution caveat (read first)\\n\\n- **This is a design-only deliverable.** No experiment has been executed, and \\u2014 given the environment (no network, no LLM API keys, no local model, no chess engine, no `python-chess`, no `numpy`/scientific Python) \\u2014 **no experiment could be executed in this workspace**. Any numerics in this document are **PARAMETERIZED \\u2014 NOT MEASURED**, clearly labelled with origin. This is consistent with `track.md`: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"*\\n- The design answers the **research question**, not a specific hypothesis about whether BoT is *worth its cost*: that is a separate accuracy-per-latency analysis and is flagged in \\u00a78 as out of scope.\\n- All non-trivial numbers carry a `Source` column: **(P)** = BoT paper mechanism / Section / Table reference, **(C)** = public third-party API pricing/latency documentation, **(I)** = illustrative assumption explicitly for the design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Research question, hypotheses (structural)\\n\\n**RQ.** Does BoT increase inference time vs single-query Expert (CoT) and single-query PAL across Game of 24, MGSM, and Checkmate-in-One?\\n\\n**Structural hypotheses (derived only from BoT's published mechanism, not from measured data).**\\n| ID | Hypothesis | Source for structure |\\n|----|------------|----------------------|\\n| H1 | Per-query wall-clock: BoT > Expert in steady state. | (P) BoT issues \\u2265 2 LLM round-trips per query; Expert issues 1. (cf. setup_7 vs setup_9) |\\n| H2 | Per-query wall-clock: BoT > PAL in steady state (under native comparability). | (P) BoT adds retrieval + 1 extra LLM round-trip vs PAL's 1 LLM round-trip. |\\n| H3 | Cold-start BoT > amortized BoT per query; gap dominated by first-fill buffer cost. | (P) Buffer pre-population is one-time; subsequent queries pay only retrieval + template instantiation. |\\n| H4 | Overhead ratio `t_BoT / t_Expert` is largest for Checkmate-in-One (longer prompts, more LLM tokens) and smallest for MGSM (compact prompts). | (P) Token count is a near-linear term in latency for autoregressive decoding. |\\n| H5 | Token cost: BoT > Expert \\u2248 PAL (input-heavy prompts for retrieval context + template). | (P) BoT prepends retrieved template to the prompt. |\\n\\nH5 \\u21d2 BoT's overhead is **structural, not implementation-driven**; even an idealized implementation is expected to obey H1\\u2013H3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Variables\\n\\n### 2.1 Independent variables (factorial, 3 \\u00d7 3)\\n- **Method** \\u2208 {Expert, PAL, BoT}\\n  - Expert = single-query CoT \\u2192 **setup_7**\\n  - PAL = single-query code-augmented \\u2192 **setup_8**\\n  - BoT = template-guided retrieval-augmented reasoning \\u2192 **setup_9**\\n- **Benchmark** \\u2208 {Game of 24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One}\\n\\n### 2.2 Dependent variables (layered; primary is bolded)\\n- **Primary: end-to-end wall-clock latency per query, in seconds, steady-state (amortized).**\\n- Secondary component decomposition (per query):\\n  - Expert \\u2192 `t_LLM_cot`\\n  - PAL \\u2192 `t_LLM_code` + `t_exec`\\n  - BoT \\u2192 `t_embed` + `t_buffer_lookup` + `t_instance_gen` + `t_LLM_final` + `t_bookkeeping`\\n- Secondary: total input + output tokens per query.\\n- Secondary: number of LLM round-trips per query.\\n- Sanity covariate (not a competing DV): accuracy (proportion of problems solved correctly per cell).\\n\\n### 2.3 Cold-start vs amortized \\u2014 both reported (\\u00a74.4)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Materials\\n\\n### 3.1 Datasets (sourced from BoT and predecessor papers)\\n\\n| Benchmark | Origin | Construction used by BoT paper | Notes |\\n|-----------|--------|--------------------------------|-------|\\n| Game of 24 | (P) Yao et al., 2023 (Tree of Thoughts) 4-number arithmetic puzzles | Standard published puzzle list; per BoT paper Section/Table on G24: subset is small but enumeration-heavy | Brute-search-friendly \\u2192 PAL has measurably non-trivial `t_exec` |\\n| MGSM | (P) Shi et al., 2022 (MGSM); subset used in BoT evaluation | Multilingual grade-school math; per-problem ground-truth integer answer | Compact prompts; short outputs |\\n| Checkmate-in-One | (P) Lichess-derived puzzle set curated for short-horizon tactical reasoning | Single-move-mate puzzles; FEN representation is the standard textual form | Longest prompts of the three; structured board representation |\\n\\nSample-size policy (per **U3**): adopt standard published problem-set sizes as documented in those papers; report **post-hoc achieved power** for the paired test rather than fixing N by power calculation. Per-problem repeat count is fixed at K = 5 (A4) to estimate sampling variance.\\n\\n### 3.2 Model deployment target (per **A2 / U2**)\\n- **Canonical** target: GPT-4-class latency profile. Treat `t_LLM \\u2248 \\u03b1 + \\u03b2 \\u00b7 tokens_out + \\u03b3 \\u00b7 tokens_in`.\\n- **Tier-swap table** provided in \\u00a77.2 for sensitivity (GPT-3.5-class, GPT-4-mini-class).\\n\\n### 3.3 BoT task-conditioned buffer (per **A5**)\\n- Per BoT paper, each benchmark gets a task-specific meta-buffer.\\n- Buffer initialization uses the paper's seed templates (problem-class \\u2192 reasoning-template). Avoid generic/random init (A5).\\n- Anchor for *template-reuse* discussion: **setup_29**.\\n\\n### 3.4 PAL execution environment (per **A8**)\\n- Sandboxed Python interpreter. Two acceptable implementations:\\n  - `bwrap --ro-bind /usr --bind /tmp --tmpfs /home --unshare-net --timeout 30 -- python3 -I -S <script>`\\n  - `subprocess.run([...], preexec_fn=...)` + `resource.setrlimit` for CPU/mem/walltime; `signal.SIGALRM` for hard kill.\\n- No network, no file writes outside `tmpfs`. Walltime cap \\u2248 30 s per script (configurable per benchmark).\\n\\n### 3.5 Checkmate-in-One representation (per **A6**)\\n- **FEN** as the default textual board state. Free-text board allowed only as sensitivity (reported separately if used). Anchor block for single-query comparators: **setup_28**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Procedure\\n\\n### 4.1 Method implementations (operational contracts)\\n\\n**Expert (setup_7).** Single LLM round-trip with a chain-of-thought prompt. No code execution, no retrieval. Timer starts at request send; timer ends at completion of response. Output is parsed; correctness judged against ground truth.\\n\\n**PAL (setup_8).** Single LLM round-trip prompting the model to emit a self-contained Python program. The program is executed in the \\u00a73.4 sandbox. Timer covers both `t_LLM_code` and `t_exec`. Output is the program's printed value; judged against ground truth.\\n\\n**BoT (setup_9 with setup_29 template reuse).**\\n1. Compute the problem's embedding vector `e` (a deterministic function of the problem text; embedding model is fixed and ideally identical to the one used in the BoT reference pipeline).\\n2. Retrieve top-`k` templates from the task-conditioned buffer by cosine similarity (parameter `k` from BoT reference, typically small).\\n3. `t_instance_gen`: an LLM call that takes the retrieved template and instantiates it with the problem-specific values (fills variables, fixes placeholders).\\n4. `t_LLM_final`: a second LLM call that takes the instantiated template and the problem to produce the final answer.\\n5. `t_bookkeeping`: retrieval orchestration (e.g., JSON marshalling, kNN latency), measured but expected to be small.\\n\\n### 4.2 Comparability (per **A7**) \\u2014 dual reporting\\n\\n| Comparability regime | Description |\\n|----------------------|-------------|\\n| **Fixed CoT budget** | All methods receive the same prompt context window allowance and same number of allowed steps. Apples-to-apples mechanism isolation. |\\n| **Native form** | Each method is run in its canonical form (BoT with retrieval + 2 LLM calls; PAL with code exec; Expert with single CoT). Reflects realistic deployment. |\\n\\nBoth regimes are computed and reported side-by-side as a sensitivity table (\\u00a77.3). The headline result section surfaces the *native form* result.\\n\\n### 4.3 Per-query timing protocol\\n- **Wall-clock** timer uses `time.perf_counter()` (Python stdlib) anchored at request-send and at response-complete (or, for component decomposition, between segment boundaries).\\n- All times logged to a per-query record: `problem_id, method, regime, repeat_idx, t_total, t_components, tokens_in, tokens_out, correct, metadata`.\\n- Per-method component decomposition is logged explicitly so the cost-tree figure can be reproduced from the log.\\n\\n### 4.4 Cold-start vs amortized handling (per **U4**)\\n\\n- **Cold-start metric**: first query against a fresh buffer triggers a one-time `t_buffer_init` (loading templates, building the index). This time is **recorded separately** and **not folded into steady-state DVs**.\\n- **Amortized metric**: for queries 2..N, the per-query latency excludes `t_buffer_init` and is the headline steady-state DV.\\n- **Reporting**: cold-start and amortized are separate columns/rows in all tables (see \\u00a77). Cold-start is *not* a confound in the headline comparison because Expert and PAL have no analogous first-query cost; it is reported as supplementary.\\n\\n### 4.5 Sampling non-determinism (per **A4**)\\n- LLM decoding uses sampling with temperature and top-p fixed across methods for a given experiment.\\n- K = 5 repeats per (problem, method, regime). Per-problem median (or mean if distribution is sufficiently tight) is the per-cell unit for paired tests.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Statistical design\\n\\n### 5.1 Pre-registered contrasts (per researcher's request \\u2014 18 rows = 3 benchmarks \\u00d7 3 method pairs \\u00d7 2 starting-states)\\n\\n| ID | Benchmark | Method A | Method B | Starting state | Test | \\u03b1 | Effect size | CI |\\n|----|-----------|----------|----------|----------------|------|---|-------------|----|\\n| C01 | G24 | Expert | PAL | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | per-problem latency ratio `t_PAL/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C02 | G24 | Expert | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C03 | G24 | PAL | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C04 | MGSM | Expert | PAL | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_PAL/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C05 | MGSM | Expert | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C06 | MGSM | PAL | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C07 | Checkmate | Expert | PAL | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_PAL/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C08 | Checkmate | Expert | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C09 | Checkmate | PAL | BoT | steady | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C10 | G24 | Expert | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C11 | G24 | PAL | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C12 | MGSM | Expert | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C13 | MGSM | PAL | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C14 | Checkmate | Expert | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_Exp` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C15 | Checkmate | PAL | BoT | cold | paired Wilcoxon | 0.05 | `t_BoT_cold/t_PAL` | BCa bootstrap, 95 % |\\n| C16 | G24 | Expert | PAL | cold | (\\u2248 steady; identical cold/ steady) | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| C17 | MGSM | Expert | PAL | cold | (\\u2248 steady) | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| C18 | Checkmate | Expert | PAL | cold | (\\u2248 steady) | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n\\nNotes. C16\\u2013C18 are listed for completeness but are degenerate (Expert and PAL have no cold-start analogue for the buffer); they are reported explicitly to avoid implying an asymmetry.\\n\\n### 5.2 Power/achievable-effect (per **U3**)\\n- Post-hoc achieved power for each contrast is reported using the Wilcoxon paired signed-rank normal approximation at the observed effect (latency ratio) and observed N per benchmark.\\n- For design-time expectations (illustrative): paired Wilcoxon with N \\u2248 30 problems per benchmark and K = 5 repeats \\u2192 ~150 paired observations per contrast can typically detect \\u2265 20\\u201330 % latency ratios at \\u03b1 = 0.05, power \\u2248 0.8. Smaller effects (e.g., < 15 %) may be underpowered at these N. **(I) Illustrative; depends on empirical effect size.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Analysis plan\\n\\n### 6.1 Per-cell descriptive table (template)\\n\\n| Cell | n problems | K | Median t_total (s) | IQR | Mean t_total (s) | SD | Token in (mean) | Token out (mean) | RT count | Accuracy (mean \\u00b1 95 % CI) |\\n|------|-----------|---|-------------------|-----|------------------|----|-----------------|------------------|----------|---------------------------|\\n\\n### 6.2 Component-decomposition table (per benchmark, in `ms`)\\n(Banner: **PARAMETERIZED \\u2014 NOT MEASURED** \\u2014 see \\u00a77.2 for source column.)\\n\\n| Benchmark | Method | t_embed | t_buffer_lookup | t_instance_gen | t_LLM_final | t_bookkeeping | t_LLM_code | t_exec | **t_total** |\\n|-----------|--------|---------|------------------|----------------|-------------|---------------|------------|--------|-------------|\\n| G24 | Expert | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* |\\n| G24 | PAL | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* |\\n| G24 | BoT | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* |\\n| MGSM | Expert | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* |\\n| MGSM | PAL | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* |\\n| MGSM | BoT | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* |\\n| Checkmate | Expert | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* |\\n| Checkmate | PAL | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* |\\n| Checkmate | BoT | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | *MS* | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | *MS* |\\n\\n*MS = empirically measured values (placeholder pending execution).*\\n\\n### 6.3 Cost-tree stacked-bar figure \\u2014 textual specification\\n\\n**Figure name.** `cost_tree_<benchmark>.png` (one figure per benchmark; in the sensitivity report, also `<regime>` and `<tier>` variants).\\n\\n**Layout.**\\n- X-axis: method \\u2208 {Expert, PAL, BoT} (categorical, three groups, evenly spaced).\\n- Y-axis: per-query wall-clock seconds, **log scale**, with gridlines at 1 s, 10 s. Y-axis label: \\\"Per-query steady-state wall-clock latency (s, log)\\\".\\n- Bars: stacked, one bar per method, bar height = `t_total`.\\n- Color stack (bottom\\u2192top):\\n  - **Expert:** single solid color \\\"LLM call\\\" (\\u03b1 + \\u03b2\\u00b7tokens).\\n  - **PAL:** bottom = \\\"Code execution\\\"; top = \\\"LLM code-emit call\\\".\\n  - **BoT:** bottom = \\\"Embedding\\\"; then \\\"Buffer lookup\\\"; then \\\"Template instantiation LLM\\\"; then \\\"Final reasoning LLM\\\"; sliver = \\\"Bookkeeping\\\".\\n- Legend to the right; one swatch per component; consistent palette across the three sub-figures.\\n- Annotations: mean `t_total` printed above each bar; ratio `t_BoT / t_Expert` printed next to the BoT bar.\\n- Expected proportions (illustrative \\u2014 **(I)**; banner applies per \\u00a77.2):\\n  - G24: PAL `t_exec` dominates (large brute-search); BoT components roughly equal except `t_LLM_final` slightly larger.\\n  - MGSM: all methods compact; `t_exec` tiny in PAL; BoT overhead concentrated in the two LLM round-trips.\\n  - Checkmate: BoT's two LLM round-trips dominate the bar; embedding/lookup are thin slices; PAL `t_exec` may be non-trivial if a chess-engine surrogate is in-voked.\\n\\n**Justification.** The cost-tree figure is *the* single artifact most directly answering the research question; each method's bar makes the structural driver of any latency ratio visible at a glance.\\n\\n### 6.4 Inferential\\n- Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test per contrast in \\u00a75.1.\\n- Effect size = per-problem latency ratio.\\n- 95 % CI via BCa bootstrap over problems (B = 10 000 resamples).\\n- Holm\\u2013Bonferroni correction within each benchmark family (3 contrasts / benchmark / regime) to control family-wise error.\\n- Achieved power reported per contrast (see \\u00a75.2).\\n\\n### 6.5 Sanity plots\\n- Accuracy \\u00d7 latency scatter (per method, per benchmark). If a method sits at near-zero accuracy, omit it from latency comparisons or annotate prominently \\u2014 latency of a broken solver is not a comparable quantity.\\n- Per-problem latency histogram (per cell), to motivate the non-parametric test choice.\\n\\n### 6.6 Template-reuse amortization sub-analysis (per **setup_29**)\\n- Within the BoT cell, partition queries by *n_reuses* of the retrieved template (how many other problems in the buffer hit a near-identical template). Compare per-query latency across `n_reuses` buckets. **Expected direction (structural):** per-query latency decreases modestly as `n_reuses` grows because (a) the same template path is cached, (b) the embedding/lookup path may short-circuit on cache hit. Report as a sub-figure alongside the main ANOVA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Parameterized reference tables\\n\\n> **PARAMETERIZED \\u2014 NOT MEASURED.** Numbers below are **order-of-magnitude reference values** derived from third-party latency documentation, BoT paper's mechanism, and reasonable assumptions. They are not empirical measurements. Source column coding: **(P)** BoT paper mechanism / Section / Table reference; **(C)** public third-party pricing/latency docs; **(I)** illustrative assumption made explicitly for this design.\\n\\n### 7.1 Tier-swap (per **U2**)\\n\\n| Tier | \\u03b1 \\u2014 per-call fixed (s) | \\u03b2_in \\u2014 per input token (ms) | \\u03b2_out \\u2014 per output token (ms) | \\u03b3 \\u2014 embedding latency (ms) | \\u03b4 \\u2014 kNN lookup (ms) | Source |\\n|------|------------------------|------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------|--------|\\n| GPT-4-class (canonical) | 0.5 \\u2013 1.2 | 0.03 \\u2013 0.06 | 0.06 \\u2013 0.12 | 50 \\u2013 200 | 1 \\u2013 50 | (C) broad public API latency bands |\\n| GPT-4-mini-class | 0.3 \\u2013 0.7 | 0.005 \\u2013 0.02 | 0.02 \\u2013 0.05 | 30 \\u2013 100 | 1 \\u2013 50 | (C) public docs |\\n| GPT-3.5-class | 0.2 \\u2013 0.5 | 0.001 \\u2013 0.005 | 0.005 \\u2013 0.02 | 20 \\u2013 80 | 1 \\u2013 50 | (C) public docs |\\n\\n(P) Per BoT paper, the dominant latency term in retrieval-augmented decoding is the per-token output term; \\u03b1 is a one-per-call overhead.\\n\\n### 7.2 Illustrative template for the headline ratio table\\n\\n| Benchmark | Method | Median t_total (s) | IQR (s) | Token out (mean) | RT count | Source |\\n|-----------|--------|---------------------|---------|-----------------|----------|--------|\\n| G24 | Expert | \\\"[Param 4\\u20138 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 2\\u20135 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 80\\u2013250]\\\" | 1 | (I) prompt \\u2248 200 tok in / 200 tok out |\\n| G24 | PAL | \\\"[Param 5\\u201330 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 5\\u201320 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 80\\u2013250]\\\" | 1 | (I) execution dominated by brute search |\\n| G24 | BoT | \\\"[Param 8\\u201318 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 4\\u201310 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 200\\u2013500]\\\" | 2 | (I) two LLM calls, retrieval adds ms-scale |\\n| MGSM | Expert | \\\"[Param 2\\u20135 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 1\\u20133 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 40\\u2013150]\\\" | 1 | (I) short prompts/outputs |\\n| MGSM | PAL | \\\"[Param 2\\u20136 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 1\\u20133 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 40\\u2013150]\\\" | 1 | (I) execution \\u2248 ms |\\n| MGSM | BoT | \\\"[Param 4\\u201310 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 2\\u20135 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 100\\u2013300]\\\" | 2 | (I) two LLM calls dominate |\\n| Checkmate | Expert | \\\"[Param 5\\u201312 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 3\\u20137 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 150\\u2013400]\\\" | 1 | (I) longer FEN+reasoning prompts |\\n| Checkmate | PAL | \\\"[Param 5\\u201315 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 3\\u20138 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 150\\u2013400]\\\" | 1 | (I) execution cost raises PAL overhead if engine subbed |\\n| Checkmate | BoT | \\\"[Param 10\\u201325 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 5\\u201312 s]\\\" | \\\"[Param 300\\u2013700]\\\" | 2 | (I) BoT bar dominated by two LLM calls |\\n\\nQuoted ranges are populated at runtime from \\u00a77.1 coefficients and per-benchmark prompt-length distributions; this template is illustrative scaffolding only.\\n\\n### 7.3 Comparability sensitivity table (native form vs fixed budget) \\u2014 template dimensions\\n\\n| Benchmark | Pair | Median ratio (native form) | Median ratio (fixed budget) | Direction agrees? | Source |\\n|-----------|------|----------------------------|------------------------------|--------------------|--------|\\n| G24 | Exp vs PAL | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\\n| G24 | Exp vs BoT | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\\n| G24 | PAL vs BoT | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\\n| MGSM | (same rows) | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\\n| Checkmate | (same rows) | *MS* | *MS* | yes/no | (runtime fill) |\\n\\nIf direction disagrees across regimes, that is a *finding* about the design's comparability assumption, not an error.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Threats to validity (per researcher's request)\\n\\n| Threat | Description | Mitigation in the design |\\n|--------|-------------|--------------------------|\\n| **No-execution caveat** | This document is design-only; no experiment has been run. The environment cannot run it. | All numerics are PARAMETERIZED with a Source column; the design is fully specified as a research protocol ready to be handed to a compute-enabled lab; no claim of empirical results is made. |\\n| **Sampling non-determinism** | LLM decoding is stochastic under sampling. Single-run latency is noisy. | K = 5 repeats per (problem, method); per-problem aggregate (median) used as the cell's paired-test unit. |\\n| **Cold-start vs amortized confounding** | BoT's first query carries buffer-fill cost; subsequent queries do not. Folding cold-start into steady-state would overestimate BoT latency; folding it into Expert/PAL has no analogue. | Reported as separate columns/rows. Cold-start contrasts are pre-registered separately (C10\\u2013C15). |\\n| **Model tier sensitivity** | Latency coefficients \\u03b1, \\u03b2 depend on the deployment target model. Conclusions might differ by tier. | Tier-swap table (\\u00a77.1) gives \\u03b1/\\u03b2 bands. Headline result reported at every tier; sign-consistency of the latency-ratio claim across tiers is the robustness criterion. |\\n| **Comparability (fixed budget vs native form)** | Fixed-budget apples-to-apples may handicap BoT (which is naturally more expensive); native form reflects deployment but breaks mechanism isolation. | Dual reporting (\\u00a77.3). If direction disagrees across regimes, report explicitly. |\\n| **Benchmark heterogeneity** | G24 (symbolic search), MGSM (numeric math), Checkmate-in-One (board reasoning) have different latency drivers; aggregating across benchmarks hides structure. | Stratified analysis per benchmark; no main effect across benchmarks reported as a single number. |\\n| **Hardware / network variance** | Wall-clock depends on deployment substrate (datacenter vs. edge, network RTT). | The design reports component decomposition as the substrate-invariant component summary; wall-clock is reported with substrate metadata alongside every number. |\\n| **Operator / orchestration overhead** | BoT's `t_bookkeeping` depends on the buffering system's implementation. A na\\u00efve Python-list `O(N)` lookup, for instance, can dominate at scale. | `t_bookkeeping` is bounded by a simple implementation note (\\u2264 O(log N) for small buffers via a flat list; FAISS-class index for larger buffers). The design specifies a fixed budget for the buffer implementation. |\\n| **PAL on chess** | PAL needs an executable form of chess reasoning; if it shells out to a stockfish-like engine, `t_exec` is large and may not be a fair native-form comparison. | For Checkmate-in-One, PAL is run in two variants: (i) generate Python that brute-searches legal moves; (ii) generate Python invoking a chess-engine surrogate inside the sandbox. Both are reported separately as a sensitivity row. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Expected-direction discussion (grounded only in BoT's published mechanism)\\n\\n> **PARAMETERIZED \\u2014 NOT MEASURED.** This section states the **direction** the design predicts, derived only from BoT's mechanism. No measured magnitudes are claimed.\\n\\n**Why BoT is expected to be slower per query than Expert and PAL.**\\n1. **Round-trip count is the dominant term.** BoT issues \\u2265 2 LLM round-trips (template instantiation + final reasoning) per query; Expert issues 1; PAL issues 1. Since per-call fixed overhead \\u03b1 is positive (typical range 0.3\\u20131.2 s per \\u00a77.1), adding a second round-trip adds at minimum \\u03b1 tokens-decoded-plus-TTFB latency. This single structural fact is sufficient to predict **H1 and H2** from \\u00a71. (Anchor: setup_9 vs setup_7, setup_8.)\\n2. **Retrieval and embedding are smaller but additive.** `t_embed + t_buffer_lookup` is expected to be on the order of tens to a few hundred milliseconds for small buffers (\\u00a77.1, \\u03b3 + \\u03b4). These components are **not** latency-dominant but appear as the lower slices of the cost-tree bar.\\n3. **Token output is the second-largest term.** The BoT pattern prepends the retrieved template to the prompt (input) and uses the template-guided LLM to fill in the answer (output). Per-benchmark token output is therefore larger than the bare Expert pattern. Combined with the second round-trip, this drives a non-trivial additional latency share.\\n\\n**Why the cold-start vs amortized gap matters.**\\n- Cold-start BoT pays `t_buffer_init` once on the first query. For small problem sets this single-query contribution to the per-query mean is non-negligible. **Amortized** BoT excludes this and is the headline metric. If a practitioner uses BoT \\\"in anger\\\" on a small workload, cold-start can dominate; on a long workload, amortized latency is the relevant number. Splitting the two makes the deployment-relevant answer unambiguous.\\n\\n**Why the per-benchmark structure differs.**\\n- **G24** is brute-search-heavy for PAL; `t_exec` is the largest PAL slice. BoT's overhead is mechanical (extra round-trip) but moderate because the LLM portion is moderate. Expect the BoT vs PAL gap to be smaller for G24 than for MGSM.\\n- **MGSM** is prompt/output-compact; LLM call latency is small in absolute terms, but BoT's second round-trip is *relatively* more visible. Expect the largest relative overhead `t_BoT / t_Expert` here.\\n- **Checkmate-in-One** has the longest prompts/outputs. BoT's prepended template inflates input tokens the most on this benchmark. Expect the largest *absolute* overhead here, but the ratio may be in the same band as MGSM because Expert is also longer.\\n\\n**Template-reuse (setup_29) sub-analysis.**\\n- For repeated problem classes, the same template is reused across many queries. Per-query overhead that is caching-invariant (TTFB, decoding the first-pass template instantiation output) remains; per-query overhead that is cache-sensitive (e.g., retrieval scoring against a hot template, lookup-step caching) drops. The design's sub-analysis (\\u00a76.6) is the right place to see whether BoT's per-query latency genuinely approaches Expert's in the long-run steady state. **Predicted direction:** modest decrease as `n_reuses` grows, but BoT does **not** approach Expert latency in absolute terms because two LLM calls remain.\\n\\n**Net expected answer to the research question.**\\n- **Steady-state per-query wall-clock:** BoT > Expert; BoT > PAL (in both regimes, sign-consistent across model tiers from \\u00a77.1).\\n- **Cold-start:** the BoT vs {Expert, PAL} gap is larger because BoT additionally carries `t_buffer_init` on the first query only.\\n- **Relative magnitudes** depend on the benchmark and model tier; the cost-tree figure is the artifact that makes this concrete.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Study-context anchors (citation block)\\n\\nThe design anchors its IV and method definitions to the following in-scope facts:\\n\\n- **setup_7** \\u2014 Expert Prompting (single-query CoT baseline). The IV's Expert level is defined here: one LLM round-trip, no retrieval, no code execution.\\n- **setup_8** \\u2014 PAL (single-query code-augmented). The IV's PAL level is defined here: one LLM round-trip produces a self-contained Python program; the program runs in a sandbox.\\n- **setup_9** \\u2014 BoT (template-guided retrieval-augmented reasoning). The IV's BoT level is defined here: embedding + buffer lookup + template-instantiation LLM call + final-reasoning LLM call.\\n- **setup_28** \\u2014 Single-query comparators block. The grouping of Expert and PAL under \\\"single-query\\\" is anchored here; informs A7's dual-regime decision and \\u00a76.3's bar grouping.\\n- **setup_29** \\u2014 BoT template-reuse block. The discussion of long-run amortization and per-query overhead reduction is anchored here; motivates \\u00a76.6's template-reuse sub-analysis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. What is **NOT** in this design (honest scoping)\\n\\nOut of scope, flagged because an unwary reader might expect them:\\n- **No accuracy-per-latency claim.** Accuracy is a sanity covariate, not a competing DV. A study of *\\\"is BoT cost-effective?\\\"* would need a different design (accuracy-per-second frontier), and is **not** the research question.\\n- **No claim of an executed experiment.** This is design-only (\\u00a70).\\n- **No measured numbers.** All numerics are PARAMETERIZED; the Source column tells you where each row came from (\\u00a77 banner).\\n- **No claim of a particular deployment substrate.** Tier-swap parameters span realistic deployment bands (\\u00a77.1).\\n- **No statistical inference without execution.** The inferential plan (\\u00a75, \\u00a76.4) is fully specified but its outputs are placeholders (`*MS*`) pending execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Compact summary block (for the researcher's quick review)\\n\\n| Item | Value |\\n|------|-------|\\n| Deliverable | Design-only research protocol + analysis plan + expected-direction discussion grounded in BoT mechanism; **no execution**; **no measured numbers** |\\n| IV | Method \\u2208 {Expert, PAL, BoT} \\u00d7 Benchmark \\u2208 {G24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One} |\\n| Primary DV | per-query wall-clock latency, steady-state, seconds |\\n| Secondary DVs | component decomposition (per method), token in/out, LLM round-trip count |\\n| Sanity covariate | accuracy |\\n| Comparability regimes | both: fixed-budget and native form (dual reporting) |\\n| Starting states | both: cold and steady-state BoT; single for Expert/PAL |\\n| Pre-registered contrasts | 18 (15 informative + 3 degenerate Expert\\u2194PAL-cold rows) |\\n| Stat test | paired Wilcoxon signed-rank, \\u03b1 = 0.05, Holm-corrected within benchmark/regime |\\n| Effect size | per-problem latency ratio; 95 % BCa bootstrap CI |\\n| Repeats | K = 5 per (problem, method, regime) |\\n| Model tiers considered | GPT-4-class (canonical), GPT-4-mini-class, GPT-3.5-class (\\u03b1/\\u03b2 bands in \\u00a77.1) |\\n| Headline artifact | cost-tree stacked-bar figure (\\u00a76.3), one per benchmark + sensitivity variants |\\n| Anchored by | setup_7, setup_8, setup_9, setup_28, setup_29 |\\n| Output numbers | none \\u2014 every numeric is PARAMETERIZED with Source column |\", \"evidence\": \"Grounded in: (a) reconnaissance above \\u2014 environment cannot execute LLM benchmarks; (b) the researcher's specific structural asks (cost-tree spec, 18-row contrast table, threats-to-validity, expected-direction from BoT mechanism only, anchors for setup_7/8/9/28/29); (c) the protocol's DV decomposition tracks the user's layered structure (wall-clock primary, components/tokens/round-trips secondary, accuracy covariate); (d) every numeric carries a PARAMETERIZED banner with a Source column coded (P) BoT paper mechanism, (C) public API latency docs, or (I) illustrative assumption; (e) the dual comparability regimes, cold/amortized split, tier-swap table, and 18 pre-registered contrasts directly reflect the researcher's A1\\u2013A10 and U1\\u2013U5 resolutions; (f) the threats-to-validity subsection explicitly covers the five concerns the researcher named plus operator/orchestration and PAL-on-chess sensitivities; (g) the expected-direction discussion cites only BoT's mechanism (round-trip count, retrieval overhead, prepended template tokens, template-reuse amortization), and the template-reuse sub-analysis is anchored to setup_29. No fabricated measured data: every empirical column is marked *MS*; every numeric range is labelled PARAMETERIZED.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1 Inference time primary definition end to end wall clock per query steady state amortized with secondary component decomposition and tokens and round trips count\", \"A2 Same LLM held constant across methods for fairness with model identity parameterized in the design\", \"A3 Paired on problem identity within each benchmark\", \"A4 K equals 5 repeats per problem method to capture sampling variance include override flag\", \"A5 BoT uses original papers task conditioned meta buffer not random or generic initialization\", \"A6 Checkmate in One uses FEN representation as default with free text board as sensitivity\", \"A7 Comparability dual reporting of fixed CoT budget and native form results as a sensitivity row\", \"A8 PAL sandbox uses bwrap or stdlib subprocess with resource and signal caps no network no fs write outside tmp walltime cap\", \"A9 Effect size equals per problem latency ratio with BCa bootstrap CI significance test equals paired Wilcoxon Holm corrected within benchmark family\", \"A10 Deliverable is pure design document no mock simulator build PARAMETERIZED numerics allowed with banner and source column\", \"U1 Pure design only no mock build\", \"U2 Canonical target is GPT 4 class latency profile with tier swap table for GPT 3.5 and GPT 4 mini\", \"U3 Standard published problem set sizes adopt with post hoc achieved power report per contrast\", \"U4 Both cold start and amortized BoT are reported in separate columns rows\", \"U5 Parameterized numerics acceptable with mandatory PARAMETERIZED banner and source column for each numeric\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1 Whether deliverable should be design only or include built mock; resolved to design only\", \"U2 Which LLM tier is the canonical target; resolved to GPT 4 class with tier swap table\", \"U3 Pre register MDE vs adopt published set and report power; resolved to published set plus post hoc power\", \"U4 Cold start vs amortized; resolved to report both\", \"U5 Parameterized numerics acceptable; resolved with mandatory PARAMETERIZED banner and source column\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b0ed8b37-adde-4a5f-bce6-5a9b59ea74f3", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 27}
